Beryllium: the status page names each kind of process that is not confined and why, and every child gives up new privileges

Purpose: plan/18 sections 4.3 and 4.7 (feature matrix S-PRC-08, S-HRD-01;
  decided 12 November 2026). A process is either confined, with tests, or
  it is named with the reason it is not, in three places: the README,
  beryllium-sandbox(7) and beryllium://sandbox. This is the third.
  - chrome/browser/ui/webui/sandbox/sandbox_internals_ui.cc: after the
    list of processes, one line for each kind that is not confined, in a
    fixed shape, "not confined: KIND | reason": the browser, a renderer
    while the lockdown is off, the GPU process, the network service, the
    storage service, the audio service, video capture and the other
    utilities. The test suite requires a line
    for every process the page lists that the kernel does not have in
    capability mode.
  - sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc: every child process that is
    not locked down sets PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL where its sandbox would
    begin, so that an exec of a set-user-ID file from it gains nothing
    (a locked-down process sets it in its lockdown). No child of the
    browser executes anything; the browser process, which starts helper
    programs, is left alone. Feature BerylliumChildNoNewPrivileges, on by
    default; a failure is logged and the process goes on.
  Of the plan's mitigations for the GPU process this is the one that
  cannot disturb a graphics driver. The others (no forking, no core files,
  no tracing, a clean environment, no connection to the X server in a
  Wayland session) each need a run on the NVIDIA driver first and are not
  in this patch.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-utility-lockdown.patch
  (the place in both files).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.22). The lockdown's code with
  InitializeSandbox() type-checks against FreeBSD 16's headers; the page's
  function was run on Linux with stand-ins and wrote the lines.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py
  test_sandbox_page_matches_the_kernel requires a statement for every
  unconfined process on a page from 0.20.3.22;
  test_children_cannot_gain_privileges asks proccontrol(1) about every
  child process.
security-review: required (sandbox reporting, process hardening)

